A||||6||burdell1||WINDSHIELD WIPER MOTOR
Z||000000||burdell1||05-28-99||07:08 AM||||I'm having a problem with my windshield wiper motor.  It keeps blowing the fuse after i put a new fuse in (however, it doesn't do it right away.) I think it might be part of a bigger problem though because whenever I have a lot of things going (radio, rear defrost, putting on the brakes, etc.) the radio has a screeching sound and my headlights flicker (like there isn't enough power.) However, my car has no problems starting and I replaced the alternator less than two years ago. Any ideas?<br>
Z||000001||theogre||05-28-99||09:25 PM||||check all the engine compartment ground straps, All of them. There are several. Make sure the ones  from the body to the block, as well as the one from the battery are ALL good. <p>Consider replacement of Both battery cables. If either is slightly screwy....... These are a biger pain than the alternator, but are much cheaper. Also replace the body/block cable if you do the mains to the battery.<p>After you've done this, if you still fry the wiper fuse, you may have a problem in the wipers. All the other symptoms point to bad grounds so do that first.
Z||000002||burdell1||05-30-99||04:10 PM||||where exactly are the engine compartment ground straps?
Z||000003||theogre||05-30-99||08:36 PM||||all over the place. dig around. there is a heavy strap for block/body, and a thin wire from the negative bat term to the body. there are several others that very in location from year to year.
Z||000004||burdell1||06-26-99||11:40 AM||||O.k. i just got my exhaust manifolds fixed and told them to check the ground straps. He said that they are fine.  Could it be the battery? However, I've only had the battery a year and the water in it is fine. 
Z||000005||DJRice||06-28-99||08:04 AM||||You probably need to replace your battery cables like ogre said.  Also, just because your wires LOOK fine does not mean that you are getting a good ground.  If this problem occurs whenever any large amount of electrical stuff is on, no matter what it is, then the wiper motor is not the problem.  <p>Did the car do this before you replaced the alternator?  Have you had to jump start the car recently becuase of a dead battery?  I ask because a screwed up regulator in the alternator could also cause some problems.  <br>(I just had to deal with that problem in my Buick over the weekend.)
Z||000006||theogre||06-28-99||11:27 PM||||DO NOT take a mech's word that the grounds are OK! EVER! On my 87 4 cyl there are at least 7(SEVEN) grounded leads on the block alone. There are at least 3 other ground joints to the body.<p>I can tell you for a fact that Very few mech's have the tools or skill to certify a good ground. Even with the tools and skill this is often a dificult task. In over 20 years of dealing with cars, if there is one single thing I hate the absobloodylute most, it's finding bad grounds.<p>You also have to carefully inspect the thin B+ (Battery +) wire. This wire is the Entire B+ supply to the car. The thick lead is the supply to the starter only. This thin lead is also part of the charging system.<p>Damage to any of the grounds Will cause some sort of problem. Just what problem depends on what wire is flipping out. If you have any doubt about the thin battery ground, or the block/body woven ground cable, replace them and make sure the anchor points are Clean! If you don't the only other ground path from block to body is the bearings of the rear hubs and the coolant, and both are bloody poor at it. The hub bearings can in fact be destroyed by doing this job.<p>Things to look for include, but aren't limmited to,,,,,<p>Corosion.<br>fraying.<br>"swollen" insulation, espesially next to the crimp on lugs. (this either means the wire is broke/breaking inside the jacket, or it coroded inside the jacket.)<br>Loose anchor points.<br>high resistance. (body/block, battery/body, or battery/block leads should show no more than fractions of an ohm.)<br>Voltage diferential. The body and block should be the same voltage. 0VDC is the ideal when a meter is connected to the block and body. 0vdc is hard to come by, but the voltage shouldn't be more than 1vdc.
